Stop gyp from rewriting arguments that are not plain paths on Windows

QuoteForRspFile() in msvs_emulation.py guesses which arguments are paths and runs
os.path.normpath() over them, because MSVC does not like foo/../bar. The guess is
"the argument contains a slash", so any argument that merely mentions a path gets
rewritten as a whole. Two build steps are damaged by that, and both only show up
with gyp's Windows ninja generator - Node's own Windows build uses MSBuild, whose
command lines never pass through this code.

1. An option that carries a path after "=", V8's inspector generator:

       --config_value protocol.path=../../deps/v8/include/js_protocol.pdl

   normpath reads "protocol.path=.." as one directory name, the ".." after it
   cancels it, and the .rsp gets "--config_value" "deps/v8/include/js_protocol.pdl".
   The code generator then finds no path entry in the config file and stops with
   "AttributeError: 'X' object has no attribute 'path'" (windows x86_64, ninja
   1130/4414, right after ICU). Fix: normalize only what follows the first "=".

2. A define whose value is a C string, OpenSSL's module directory:

       -DMODULESDIR="D:\134a\134...\out\Release/obj/lib/openssl-modules"

   The \134 groups are gyp's own escaped backslashes (PRODUCT_DIR_ABS_CSTR) and the
   tail uses forward slashes, which is a perfectly good string for the program to
   read at run time. normpath turns the tail into single backslashes and the
   compiler then reads broken escape sequences inside the string literal:

       crypto/cversion.c(121,33): error: expected '{' after '\o' escape sequence
       FAILED: [code=1] obj/deps/openssl/openssl/crypto/openssl.cversion.obj
       FAILED: [code=1] obj/deps/openssl/openssl/crypto/openssl.defaults.obj
       (windows x86_64, ninja 1702/4234)

   Fix: leave an argument that contains a double quote exactly as written. Nothing
   needs Windows path spelling inside a string the program interprets itself.

Checked by handing ntpath.normpath to a copy of the function on Linux (the
heuristic only changes anything on Windows), before and after the patch:

    -DMODULESDIR="...\Release/obj/lib/openssl-modules"
        gyp:   ...Release\obj\lib\openssl-modules    (broken escapes in the string)
        ours:  ...Release/obj/lib/openssl-modules    (unchanged)
    protocol.path=../../deps/v8/include/js_protocol.pdl
        gyp:   deps\v8\include\js_protocol.pdl       (the name= prefix is gone)
        ours:  protocol.path=..\..\deps\v8\include\js_protocol.pdl
    arguments that really are paths behave exactly as before the patch:
        -I../deps/v8/include        -> -I..\deps\v8\include
        /Foobj/deps/v8/x.obj        -> \Foobj\deps\v8\x.obj
        -DOPENSSLDIR="C:\\Program\ Files..."  (untouched in both)

--- a/tools/gyp/pylib/gyp/msvs_emulation.py
+++ b/tools/gyp/pylib/gyp/msvs_emulation.py
@@ -30,9 +30,27 @@
     # works more or less because most programs (including the compiler, etc.)
     # use that function to handle command line arguments.
 
-    # Use a heuristic to try to find args that are paths, and normalize them
-    if arg.find("/") > 0 or arg.count("/") > 1:
-        arg = os.path.normpath(arg)
+    # Use a heuristic to try to find args that are paths, and normalize them.
+    # GodotJS: an argument that carries a double quote is left alone. Those are
+    # usually a define whose value is a C string the program interprets itself -
+    # OpenSSL is compiled with
+    # -DMODULESDIR="D:\134a\...\Release/obj/lib/openssl-modules",
+    # where \134 is gyp's own escaped backslash - and normalizing it turns the
+    # forward slashes into single backslashes, which the compiler then reads as
+    # broken escape sequences inside the string literal.
+    if '"' not in arg and (arg.find("/") > 0 or arg.count("/") > 1):
+        # GodotJS: for a name=value argument, normalize only the value. Running
+        # normpath over the whole argument treats "protocol.path=.." as a
+        # directory, which the following ".." cancels, so V8's inspector
+        # --config_value protocol.path=../../include/js_protocol.pdl came out as
+        # ../../include/... with the "protocol.path=" gone (and then the code
+        # generator fails with "'X' object has no attribute 'path'").
+        eq = arg.find("=")
+        slash = arg.find("/")
+        if 0 < eq < slash:
+            arg = arg[: eq + 1] + os.path.normpath(arg[eq + 1 :])
+        else:
+            arg = os.path.normpath(arg)
 
     # For a literal quote, CommandLineToArgvW requires 2n+1 backslashes
     # preceding it, and results in n backslashes + the quote. So we substitute
